                <td class="p-3 font-tibetan font-bold text-accent" lang="bo">མཐིང་། (Mthing)</td>
                <td class="p-3">Deep ultramarine midnight blue</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-medium text-ink">Malachite</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-tibetan font-bold text-accent" lang="bo">སྤང་མ། (Spang ma)</td>
                <td class="p-3">Rich mountain emerald green</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-medium text-ink">Cinnabar</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-tibetan font-bold text-accent" lang="bo">མཚལ། (Mtshal)</td>
                <td class="p-3">Vivid vermilion cinnabar red</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-medium text-ink">Orpiment</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-tibetan font-bold text-accent" lang="bo">བ་བླ། (Ba bla)</td>
                <td class="p-3">Opaque warm solar yellow</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-medium text-ink">Refined Gold Leaf</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-tibetan font-bold text-accent" lang="bo">གསེར་ཆུ། (Gser chu)</td>
                <td class="p-3">Luminous gold, agate-stone burnished</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-muted font-serif leading-relaxed m-0 pt-1">
          After completing the divine garments, artists burnish the dried gold details with an agate-tipped stylus (<em>gzi</em>), bringing out a radiant sheen that reflects butter lamp light in the darkness of the temple shrine.
        </p>
      </div>
